# Shared lists and dicts

Lists and dicts are `Rc<RefCell<_>>`. Assignment and `$a[] = $b` store that same buffer. `$b = $a; $b[] = 1` changes `$a`. `$a[] = $a` is a cycle. `strict_eq` returns true when both sides are the same buffer before it walks children. Two different cyclic values may overflow comparison.

A list may hold scalars, lists, and dicts together. A list or dict used as a set element or hashmap key is a type error. `clone $a` is the deep copy.

List indexes are dense `0..=len`. `$a[] =` appends. An index past `len` is a type error. Dict keys are strings. Any other key on an empty array becomes a hashmap. Untyped `[]` stays `EmptyArray` until the first write.

`unset($x[$k])` is not supported. Delete with `$x->remove(...)`. `unset($var)` still clears the variable. `list->remove` takes an int index. `dict->remove` takes a string key. `set->remove` takes a value. `hashMap->remove` takes a key. A tuple has a fixed length and no `remove`. List and dict `remove` warn because they shift. Set and hashmap do not.

## RHP code

```php
list $a = [1];
list $b = $a;
$b[] = 2;
print($a[1]); // 2

$a[] = $a;
print($a == $a); // true

dict $node = ['name' => 'Ada'];
$node['parent'] = $node;
$node->remove('name'); // warning: dict removal shifts

list $items = [1, 2];
$items->remove(0); // warning: list removal shifts

$set = Arr::set([1]);
$set->remove(1); // no warning

unset($items[0]); // error: use ->remove()
```
